Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.929 align:start position:0%
 
Desiree you and your co-writer Cecilia  adapted this film from a novel by Emily  Danforth I'm curious as to how you found  the book and how you guys made the  decision to focus the film largely on  basically the second half of Emily's  novel yeah so I read the book when it  first came out a publisher friend sent  it to me and thought I would like it and  I loved it and I shared it with every  woman in my life and I've seen make it  so gendered I mean every lesbian in my  life and I shared it with my girlfriend  at the time who loved it too and said  you need to make this into a movie and  my incident thought was fear because the  book was so good and the tone was so  pitch perfect it danced beautifully  between tragedy and comedy and to me it  was the first really honest depiction of  teen life I'd read in a book and it  reminded me of John Hughes film and the  teen films I had grown up on and I was  really intimidated by that task because  you know 80% of the work I had done like  consisted of mainly fart jokes so I put  it aside for a while and then I made my  first feature film which was you know  super low budget and put together with  like spit and good intentions and favors  from friends and then I after when I was  traveling on the festival circuit with  that film my producing partner said what  do you want to do next and I was like  okay not next this is a book read it  love it and then in ten years  let's earned the right to make it and  she read it and she said we're doing  this and got the rights and we wrote it  on spec I mean I can only imagine how  hard it must be just in general to  distill a novel into a 90-minute film  you asked that how you decided to kind  of just look at the second half of the  book ya know from the get-go I knew I  didn't want to focus on the whole book I  had no interest in telling so the books  start the books 500 pages long and it  starts when Cameron's 11 and it ends  when she's 17 and the last 200  pages take place at God's promise the  center she sent - I always knew I wanted  to focus on that just the dramatic  archive it was so much more condensed I  hate biopics I hate it when movies go  and yeah it just feels like a watered  down version of someone's whole life I  knew that I liked I loved a queen in the  way that was condensed I wanted to make  a very condensed narrative that focused  on one very specific moment in this  girl's life that was emblematic of what  it was to be a teenager  Kolak would you have been ready to play  aged 11 had Desiree decided to do the  book from start to finish for each of  you what do you think it means to the  film that it does take place 25 years  ago obviously it's pre social media  which i think is really interesting but  for how do you think that informs the  film or changes the film that it's 1993  as opposed to 2013 well for me I just  wanted to keep them as isolated as  possible like the book takes place in 93  and it would have been more affordable  for us to shoot it now but at the end of  the day if they had any connection to  the outside world or any idols it would  have changed the feeling they're so  alone they have no idea of what life  could look like as a functioning  homosexual and I was important to me I  grew up Iranian with a conservative  Iranian parents and when I came out of  the closet it was like coming out as a  frickin leprechaun parents were like  what is that Medina's odd the president  at the time had just publicly announced  that there were no it was in Iran yeah  you went to Columbia and he was like  that doesn't exist in my country it is  it's an ailment that's punishable by  stoning to this day and it was so  unheard of and that was the loneliness  of coming out of never having seen  someone who looked like me who was like  me and that was one of the elements of  the book that I love that I related to  so deeply so to change it to today would  have changed the meaning entirely and  obviously as an actor you're doing  period different periods all the time  Chloe but for you was what did you do to  explore a time frame that shockingly you  were not even the wife 25 years ago I  think you think more than then more than  just trying to graph it grasp a time  period I found it very interesting the  the juxtaposition of where we were  politically in America when we filmed  this movie compared to the 1993 setting  and how when we assign it when I signed  on to this movie we were living in Obama  era still it's a very different climate  in America I've always been a you know  an advocate for the LGBT community so  this was something that was very  important to me but in so many ways  you know we were a champion and movie  that didn't have the societal impact  that it did halfway through the project  when Donald Trump was elected and all of  a sudden I realized that not only were  we doing a beautiful piece that I think  depicts being a young gay person if for  the first time very adequately this is a  societally impactful rebellious movie  this is the highest form of activism  that you could do and the fact that it's  2018 and not much has changed when you  think back to the filming I mean the  cast is you've got these Tony winners  like Jennifer and John Gallagher you  have these people relative newcomers  like Forrest and Sasha  you've got Chloe Chloe when you think  about to the filming who kept you on  your toes the most of your fellow cast  members was there like a wild card I  think it was it was the perfect example  of an ensemble because we we didn't have  enough rehearsals to really know what we  were going into we just had to jump in  and we all did our rehearsals you know  the night before alone and then we had a  very finite amount of time in our data  shoot it so there was an energy that was  almost like being on stage with each  other and it was special because working  with someone like Sasha working with  someone like Forrest who were the same  age as me but I've I've acted for 15  years and  having their energy come in in a lot of  ways it it broke a self-awareness that I  have just naturally from being very  comfortable in front of the camera it  broke it down so they're not hitting  their marks and we're not you know we're  not doing things as strategically as  they would be done if you're with a  bunch of seasoned doctors which i think  is really important and it comes across  very organic and I think you can really  feel that in the film it's  unconventional and so I think that also  goes for Ashley Connor in the way that  she shot us it was very unconventional  you know we didn't have a locked-off  shot it wasn't you know when  establishing and then we go closer and  closer and closer it was let's just turn  over here and get this shot I felt like  everything was dictated by the tools in  our arsenal you know we were shooting at  a very little budget with a very  restricted amount of time with actors  who Chloe's done 60 films sasha has done  one she shot it in a van and she you  know when you tell her like you know  when you give her a specific instruction  she's like come again I mean she's great  like she figured it out and by the end  we were running like clockwork but at  the same time it was such a discrepancy  between all of us and our skill set but  also with our money and our time so what  was I mean this is my favorite way to  work to is sort of at the top the dasie  like all right these these are the pages  we need to accomplish these are the  people around us and these are the  problems you know we're missing these  props this weather problems happen you  know like you just make a checklist of  the problems and you're like what soup  can I make with these ingredients it's  my favorite way of cooking as well  you're like this is what's in the fridge  what am I gonna make and that's what I  mean a lot of it lives in the close-up  because Chloe's performance dictated  that because Ashley's handheld is like  butter and she could maneuver it quite  nicely you see like and that's one thing  I really love about directing is you're  a bit of a like a conductor you're like  okay your skill in art is in my art  department is like oh you do really good  props and like oh I saw your  clinton-gore graphic great let's use  that like you know everyone has  something else that they can bring to  the table and you get to decide where  the talent goes and when to bring it  down well this is a movie I think is  going to help  a lot of people so I congratulate both  of you Desiree and Chloe thank you so  much for being here

